          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When someone says they have heart failure, does that mean the heart has stopped working?",
          "answer": "Heart failure does not mean that the heart has stopped working. Instead, it is a clinical syndrome in which the heart cannot fill with or eject blood effectively enough to meet the body's needs at normal pressure levels. This condition is often chronic and managed over many years (source: \"Heart failure\").\n\nHeart failure is classified based on how well the heart pumps blood (known as ejection fraction) and can vary in severity and type. It can be a complex condition that requires ongoing management and does not imply an immediate danger of the heart stopping (source: \"Coding heart failure: I50, its mechanism axis and its acuity axis\"). \n\nI could not cover specific symptoms or treatment details, as the information does not provide that level of detail.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
